  "tool.statistics.note.ignored": "Not read (skipped):",
  "tool.statistics.note.spread": "Both spreads are shown side by side: the sample spread (denominator n−1) and the population spread (denominator n). Which one applies depends on the question — so neither is withheld.",
  "tool.statistics.note.quartile": "Quartiles by linear interpolation (type 7, as QUARTILE in spreadsheets). Other methods give other numbers.",
  "tool.statistics.note.mode": "A most frequent value is only reported when it really repeats.",
  "tool.statistics.formulas": "Mean: x̄ = Σx / n. Median and quartiles: p-quantile at position (n−1)·p with linear interpolation. Spread: s² = Σ(x − x̄)² / n or / (n−1) — computed as the sum of squared deviations, not with the short form Σx² − n·x̄², which becomes unusable for large numbers with small spread. Outlier fences: Q1 − 1.5·IQR and Q3 + 1.5·IQR. Regression: slope b = Sxy / Sxx, intercept a = ȳ − b·x̄, correlation r = Sxy / √(Sxx·Syy).",
  "tool.statistics.assumptions": "Assumptions: numbers are read from free text (spaces, commas, semicolons, line breaks); a comma between digits is the decimal separator. Unreadable parts are reported, not silently dropped. The sample spread is undefined for exactly one value (division by zero) and stays empty. Regression needs at least two pairs with different x values. Computation is floating point.",
  "tool.statistics.sources": "Own formulas, no statistics library: all summary statistics and the regression formulas are standard methods of descriptive statistics. Verified against independent recomputation in the test suite (spread, correlation, quartiles). No new dependency. As of 2026-10-03.",
