  "tool.metal.error.profile": "These values do not give a usable cross-section.",
  "tool.metal.assumptions": "Assumptions: the cross-section is computed as an area in square millimetres and the mass from the basic formula m = F · L · ρ / 1000 (F in mm², L in metres, ρ in kg/dm³). Tubes are computed from the difference of outer and inner area, not from an approximation. For the angle the area is computed without fillets, so the result is slightly below the catalogue weight. Density is a suggested value per material and can be overwritten; alloy composition, tolerances and scale may differ. Cross-check: solid steel with a diameter of 100 mm weighs about 61.7 kg per metre.",
  "tool.metal.sources": "Sources: the basic formula, the per-profile formulas and the usual standard value for steel of 7,850 kg/m³ (witte-tube.com, abtersteel.com); density table with aluminium 2.70, brass 8.5 and copper 8.96 (hug-technik.com, edelstahlrohrshop.com); stainless steel 1.4301 (304) 7.9 kg/dm³ (mirrorinox.de); cast iron 7.25 (handwerkerwerkzeuge concept, sourced there). Bronze, zinc, lead and titanium are marked as rule-of-thumb values. No material standard and no guarantee of delivery weight — the result is a theoretical calculation from geometry.",
  "tool.metal.roundNote": "Values are computed to twelve significant digits and displayed in the user’s language. The result is a theoretical weight; delivery weights may differ through tolerances.",
