  "tool.wood.error.mass": "The mass must be between 0.001 and 100,000 kilograms.",
  "tool.wood.error.dryHeavier": "The oven-dry mass must not exceed the wet mass — dry wood weighs less than wet wood.",
  "tool.wood.error.moisture": "The wood moisture must be between 0 and 300 percent.",
  "tool.wood.error.density": "The density must be between 0.05 and 1.5 kg/dm³.",
  "tool.wood.error.count": "The number of pieces must be between 1 and 100,000.",
  "tool.wood.error.dimension": "The dimensions must be between 1 and 12,000 millimetres.",
  "tool.wood.error.length": "The length must be between 0.01 and 100 metres.",
  "tool.wood.assumptions": "Assumptions: wood moisture is a mass percentage referred to the oven-dry mass, exactly as the trade literature defines it: u = (wet mass − oven-dry mass) / oven-dry mass · 100. Density applies at a wood moisture of 12 to 15 percent, as it settles indoors. It is deliberately not converted to other moisture levels: swelling also changes the volume, so such a conversion would be false precision. Density varies considerably within a species by growing region and ring width — the table therefore gives averages that can be overwritten.",
  "tool.wood.sources": "Average densities at 12 to 15 percent wood moisture: Bavarian State Institute of Forestry (LWF), table of Central European timbers, reproduced at sanier.de; cross-check in the LWF series \"Wissen 57\" (pine 0.52, spruce 0.47, fir 0.47, larch 0.59, Douglas fir 0.58, oak 0.67–0.69, beech 0.69–0.72 g/cm³). Moisture formula and range classification (0–6 dry, 6–35 working range, 35 and above wet): bauhandwerk.de and holzland.de. No statement about standards and no drying instruction — measure on the component, do not calculate.",
  "tool.wood.roundNote": "Values are computed to twelve significant digits and displayed in the user’s language. Density is an average; the result is a pre-calculation, not a weighing check.",
